  "id": "DrugBank_DB00588 - MONDO_15758",
  "verbalization": "Fluticasone Propionate, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Primary Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (PCTCL) through a mechanism involving the indirect action of its metabolites. The evidence suggests that Fluticasone Propionate may act through a chain of indications that ultimately target the disease process. Specifically, it is linked to Granuloma Annulare and Contact Dermatitis, both of which are associated with Desoximetasone, a corticosteroid with similar pharmacological properties. Desoximetasone, in turn, is linked to PCTCL, indicating a potential role in the disease progression. The shared pharmacological profile of these corticosteroids suggests a possible mechanism of action involving anti-inflammatory or immunosuppressive effects, which could modulate the immune response and contribute to the development of PCTCL. The evidence is supported by a consistent pattern of indications across different pathways, reinforcing the hypothesis that corticosteroids may play a role in the pathogenesis of PCTCL through mechanisms involving immune modulation and inflammation.",
